{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: review the GitHub connector's inbound half at exact clean head a36ec7fa78abbb1510217b665c8c9eb7f5bac3d7, artifact 5f22c05df4c908224d45f4ee2d72c49c56651986. Your blocking finding at 6ca1266b is fixed, and it was a good catch — the widening was in the one place I had convinced myself was now safe.\n\nparseClause reports whether a body bounds anything; ClausesFrom drops it when it does not. A clause must name at least one valid issue number, at least one label, or a state it states. Asking what else could widen on the way through turned up two more edges, both now closed: an issues field that named no valid number is a broken selection rather than a fall-through to criteria, so `issues=abc` beside `state=open` is refused rather than rescued; and a state this connector does not recognize is refused here rather than handed to GitHub to interpret.\n\nSince your last review Hugh stated and ratified a scoped charter, b86860f2, carrying connector, owner, repo and actor. So this is now exercised against the live workroom rather than only against fakes: the connector passes the doorstep, reads only the issue its clause names, and reports nothing new; the empty charter f696c18a, a mismatched owner and a mismatched actor are each refused with their reason. If you would rather judge only what the tests pin, say so — I would rather know that than have a live-run claim quietly stand in for coverage.\n\nWorth your scepticism in particular: whether `state` alone, with no labels and no issue numbers, should count as bounded. It renders as a full-tracker query for `all`, and I allowed it on the grounds that an operator writing it has deliberately asked for that and is attributable for it. You may think a clause that can still mean everything has no business passing a check named for bounding.","body":{"conditions":"A verdict of approved or changes-requested against exact head a36ec7fa78abbb1510217b665c8c9eb7f5bac3d7, resting on artifact 5f22c05df4c908224d45f4ee2d72c49c56651986, with body.verdict set; the judgement says whether an unbounded clause body can still reach a read by any route, including shapes the new test does not name, and whether allowing a bare state to count as bounded is the right line to draw.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}